p1. the two refuting rows are constructional, and the question that can fail
==============================================================================

PART ONE. Mutate the index-4 target's encoding and watch the row not move.

  index-4 VALUE ops, target encoding twos_complement : 256/256
  index-4 VALUE ops, target encoding excess_8        : 256/256
  index-4 VALUE ops, target encoding BROKEN_constant : 256/256

  The third row's target encoding maps all sixteen values onto one pattern and is not
  injective, not surjective, and not an encoding of anything. The check does not notice,
  because it never calls it.

  And at index 5, the same, with the housing offset swept:
    index-5 VALUE ops, target offset 0: 256/256
    index-5 VALUE ops, target offset 1: 256/256
    index-5 VALUE ops, target offset 2: 256/256
    index-5 VALUE ops, target offset 4: 256/256

  VERDICT: both rows are true by construction. `71`'s refutation of `67:252` STANDS,
  and its evidence is a structural fact rather than a 4-bit measurement. Cite it as
  'by construction, at any width' and the word 'never' in `71`'s X2 is licensed.

==============================================================================
PART TWO. The index-4 question a consumer feels: operate on the stored bits, read back.

  via_patterns(a, b) := load(raw_add(store a, store b)), where raw_add is the
  container's own wrapping add on the four stored bits. Compared against the system's
  own value-level add. This CAN fail and does.

  window     policy     encoding                    via-patterns == value add
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  signed     wrap       natural (two's complement)             256/256
  signed     wrap       biased (excess-8)                        0/256
  signed     saturate   natural (two's complement)             192/256
  signed     saturate   biased (excess-8)                        1/256
  unsigned   wrap       natural (identity)                     256/256
  unsigned   wrap       biased (rotate-8)                        0/256
  unsigned   saturate   natural (identity)                     136/256
  unsigned   saturate   biased (rotate-8)                        8/256

  So a re-encoding cannot change what the crossing computes on VALUES, and it can and
  does change what the same bits compute when a consumer adds them without consulting
  the type. Those are two different sentences about index 4 and the panel has been
  writing one of them.

  under wrapping the natural encoding agrees at 256/256 signed and 256/256 unsigned;
  the biased one at 0/256 and 0/256.
  That is `63` section 3.5's constant defect, arriving as a number a consumer can feel
  rather than as a property of a crossing square: a biased encoding forfeits the raw
  adder totally, at every operand pair, in both sign domains.

  What this does not establish: any magnitude, anything past four bits, and nothing
  about whether either encoding should be preferred. It replaces one vacuous row with
  one that can fail.
